Falsifies `paging::x86_64::the_indices_and_offset_tile_the_address`.

The defect: the levels are numbered from the leaf instead of from the root. Level 0 now selects
bits 20:12 and level 3 bits 47:39, which is exactly the walk running upside down. This is the
most plausible of the three defects recorded against `index`, because the two conventions both
exist in the literature: Intel numbers its tables PML4, PDPT, PD, PT from the root, while the
shift arithmetic is most naturally written counting up from the page. The walk in `Mapper`
descends from 0, so this defect makes it descend through the tables in reverse.

Expected to fail: `assert_eq!(reconstructed, va & 0x0000_ffff_ffff_ffff)`. The harness
reassembles the address with the four literal shifts 39, 30, 21, 12 against `index(va, 0..3)`,
so a reversed numbering swaps the outer pair with the inner pair and the reconstruction differs
from `va` for any address whose top and bottom index fields are not equal.

**The index math is shared and the proof is not.** The `aarch64` and `sv39` records break the
same default method in `lib.rs` two other ways.

diff --git a/crates/paging/src/lib.rs b/crates/paging/src/lib.rs
--- a/crates/paging/src/lib.rs
+++ b/crates/paging/src/lib.rs
@@ -477,7 +477,7 @@ pub trait PageFormat {
     /// covers the highest translated bits; each lower level shifts down by 9. Derived from
     /// [`LEVELS`](Self::LEVELS) so it is right for both a 4- and a 3-level walk.
     fn index(va: u64, level: usize) -> usize {
-        let shift = 9 * (Self::LEVELS - 1 - level) as u32 + 12;
+        let shift = 9 * level as u32 + 12;
         ((va >> shift) & 0x1ff) as usize
     }
 
